  volume = {97},
  number = {11},
  pages = {8233-8239},
  month = {DEC 1},
  abstract = {A flow tube apparatus was used to investigate the formation of SO5- ions by gas phase ion-molecule reactions. The reactions studied in an N2 buffer gas at 2.5 hPa pressure and room temperature (298 K) included SO2 and O2 reactions with O2-, O3-, CO3-, SO2-, and SO3-well as their hydrates. Reaction rate constants were measured and the major product channels were identified for most reactions. The free energy changes for the hydration reactions of SO3-, SO4-, and SO5- were derived from equilibrium constant measurements. The present investigations clearly show that SO5- ions are formed in the gas phase by the association of O2 to SO3- and by the switching reaction of SO3-H2O with O2. An effective binary rate constant of 2.0 X 10(-12) cm3 s-1 was measured for the association reaction at 2.5 hPa N2 and the rate constant of the switching reaction was 5.0 x 10(-11) cm3 s-1. Also the reaction of O3-H2O with SO2 probably yields SO5- by a switching process having a rate constant of 1.8 x 10(-9) cm3 s-1. The heat of formation of SO5- was estimated to be less than -715 kJ/mol. The present results have implications to the negative ion chemistry of the atmosphere and are important for measurements of atmospheric SO2 concentrations by chemical ionization mass spectrometry.},
